% Regressions from the M1..M3 code review. Each of these passed the suite as it
% stood and was wrong anyway, so each is pinned by the smallest case that shows
% the wrong answer rather than by the code path that produced it.

### name: review.count.scalar-has-no-children
--- note
Was 1. `$base + ['scalarRule' => true]` — PHP's array union keeps the LEFT
operand's value for a duplicated key, so the flag was permanently false and
COUNT of a scalar counted the one-element list §7.3 wraps it in. spec §7.4 says
COUNT is the number of children, and a scalar has none.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
COUNT("hello")
--- expect
0
===
### name: review.count.scalar-column-has-no-children
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "column": "c", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
COUNT(C) == 0
--- expect
(0 = 0)
===
### name: review.has.scalar-has-no-keys
--- note
Was TRUE, from the same flag.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
HAS("hello", 1)
--- expect
FALSE
===
### name: review.relation.fields-are-case-insensitive
--- note
Was E_SQL_BINDING. Bindings normalised the field names and then returned
`$b + ['fields' => $fields]`, which kept the host's raw lowercase keys — the
same union trap. Every consumer looks up strtoupper(), and every fixture in
12-aggregates happened to be written uppercase.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "t", "alias": "oi", "fields": {"qty": {"table": "oi", "column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ANY(ITEMS, I, I["QTY"] > 100)
--- expect
EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM `t` `oi` WHERE TRUE AND ((`oi`.`qty` > 100)) IS TRUE)
===
### name: review.value.num-binding-must-be-a-number
--- note
Was an injection: a NUM-typed value binding is the one literal emitted without
quotes, and it reached the emitter straight from host data. "1 OR 1=1 -- " went
out verbatim. The part list stops a literal being confused with SQL; it cannot
stop a literal from BEING SQL.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "column": "c", "type": "NUM"}, "V": {"kind": "value", "type": "NUM", "value": "1 OR 1=1 -- "}}
--- source
C == V
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: review.value.num-binding-checks-children-too
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"L": {"kind": "value", "type": "NUM", "value": {"1": "1", "2": "2) OR (1=1"}}}
--- source
ALL(L, _ > 0)
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: review.value.legitimate-num-binding-still-works
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "column": "c", "type": "NUM"}, "V": {"kind": "value", "type": "NUM", "value": "100"}}
--- source
C == V
--- expect
(`c` = 100)
===
### name: review.regex.empty-flags-are-not-the-i-flag
--- note
Was case-insensitive. The template was chosen by argument count, so every
three-argument call got (?si) — including the ordinary "pass empty flags
through" one, where SEL is case-sensitive.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "column": "c", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
RMATCH("abc", C, "")
--- expect
(`c` COLLATE utf8mb4_bin REGEXP '(?s)abc')
===
### name: review.regex.i-flag-selects-the-other-template
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "column": "c", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
RMATCH("abc", C, "i")
--- expect
(`c` COLLATE utf8mb4_bin REGEXP '(?si)abc')
===
### name: review.regex.unknown-flag-is-refused
--- note
Compiled happily to (?si) before. The evaluator raises E_BAD_ARG on it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "column": "c", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
RMATCH("abc", C, "zzz")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: review.regex.i-flag-needs-an-ascii-pattern
--- note
Case folding above ASCII is the one thing PCRE and ECMAScript cannot be made to
agree on, so the evaluator refuses this. A translation that accepted it would
disagree with the host that refused it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "column": "c", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
RMATCH("zażółć", C, "i")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: review.register.a-skeleton-is-reachable
--- note
Was silently dead: the overlay upper-cased every key, but skeleton names are
camel-case and looked up verbatim, so a registered skeleton went somewhere
nothing reads. This is the documented escape hatch for exactly the JOIN the
shipped map refuses.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "skel", "join", {"tpl": "(SELECT GROUP_CONCAT({body} ORDER BY {body} SEPARATOR {sep}) FROM {from} WHERE {corr})"}]}]
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "t", "alias": "r", "scalar": "S", "fields": {"S": {"table": "r", "column": "s", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
JOIN(R, ",")
--- expect
(SELECT GROUP_CONCAT(`r`.`s` ORDER BY `r`.`s` SEPARATOR ',') FROM `t` `r` WHERE TRUE)
===
### name: review.bindings.raw-from-without-an-alias
--- note
Raised a TypeError, not a SqlError — so tryTranslate() did not catch it and a
host using the refusal-tolerant API got a fatal instead of null.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "relation", "from": {"raw": "(SELECT 1)"}, "fields": {}}, "B": {"kind": "relation", "from": {"raw": "(SELECT 2)"}, "fields": {}}}
--- source
1 + 1
--- expect
(1 + 1)
===
### name: review.bindings.raw-from-alias-collision-still-caught
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "relation", "from": {"raw": "(SELECT 1)"}, "fields": {}}, "B": {"kind": "relation", "from": {"raw": "(SELECT 1)"}, "fields": {}}}
--- source
1 + 1
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: review.in.value-binding-holding-a-list
--- note
Refused before, though §5.4 says a value list unrolls exactly like a literal
one — and an allow-list is the most natural thing a host binds this way.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"S": {"kind": "column", "column": "s", "type": "TEXT"}, "ALLOWED": {"kind": "value", "value": {"1": "open", "2": "held"}}}
--- source
S IN ALLOWED
--- expect
((CAST(`s` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('open' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin) OR (CAST(`s` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('held' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin))
===
### name: review.params.reordering-template-binds-in-output-order
--- note
FIND(needle, hay) maps to INSTR({1}, {0}), so the second slot created is the
first emitted. A positional ? carries no number, so bindings() must walk the
output — returning them in creation order bound the hay to the needle's
placeholder. Verified against MariaDB 11.8 under native prepares.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- mode
params
--- source
FIND("a", "banana")
--- expect
INSTR(?, ?)
--- params
t"banana", t"a"
### name: kinds.branches-must-agree
--- note
Found by the fuzz lane; pinned here because the mutation runner showed nothing
else would notice if the refusal were removed. SEL answers the BOOL TRUE, whose
text is "TRUE"; the CASE answers 1. Nothing casts one to the other and no caveat
says "a boolean becomes 1".
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
IF(TRUE, TRUE, "A-1")
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.unknown-still-unifies-with-anything
--- note
The other half of the rule. UNKNOWN is what an undeclared column type is for,
and it must keep unifying, or every mixed branch over a plain column would be
refused.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "column": "c"}}
--- source
IF(TRUE, C, "x")
--- expect
CASE WHEN TRUE THEN `c` ELSE 'x' END
===
### name: kinds.bool-is-not-byte-comparable-with-a-number
--- note
Found by the fuzz lane; pinned here for the same reason. CAST(0 AS CHAR) and
CAST(FALSE AS CHAR) are both '0', so NOT (0 IN FALSE) was TRUE in SEL and FALSE
on the server. EQL and IN are structural: a kind mismatch is FALSE forever.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
0 IN FALSE
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.two-bools-compare-fine
--- note
'1' against '0' is exactly right, so the refusal must not be wider than the
defect.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
TRUE EQL FALSE
--- expect
(CAST(TRUE AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST(FALSE AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: kinds.an-unknown-operand-is-the-accepted-limit
--- note
Where the binding did not declare a type, nothing here can decide either. That
is the limit, and it passes rather than guessing.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "column": "c"}}
--- source
C EQL TRUE
--- expect
(CAST(`c` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST(TRUE AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: kinds.bool-is-not-byte-comparable-directly
--- note
The same rule on the other path. `IN` is lowered by inOperator and $==/EQL by
binary(), and the mutation runner found that only one of the two was pinned:
removing the guard from binary() survived every check.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
0 EQL FALSE
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.bool-is-not-byte-comparable-with-text
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"x" $== TRUE
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.a-bool-is-not-a-number
--- note
`0 != FALSE` is E_NOT_NUM in SEL -- not FALSE. MariaDB and SQLite coerce a
boolean to 1 or 0 and answer anyway, which is a wrong answer with no error
attached; PostgreSQL says `cannot cast type boolean to numeric` and fails the
query. Refusing is the only outcome that matches SEL, and it took a
strongly-typed server to make the gap visible.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
0 != FALSE
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.a-bool-is-not-an-addend
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
1 + TRUE
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.a-bool-cannot-be-negated
--- note
The unary path takes the same guard.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
-(TRUE)
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.an-unknown-column-still-adds
--- note
The limit of the rule: UNKNOWN passes, because the binding did not say and
nothing here can either.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"C": {"kind": "column", "column": "c"}}
--- source
C + 1
--- expect
(`c` + 1)
===
### name: kinds.bin-is-not-text-comparable-under-eql
--- note
SEL's EQL and IN compare kinds first, and a SEL number IS a text value, so the
classes are {NUM, TEXT}, {BOOL} and {BIN}. The guard compared only BOOL-ness,
so BIN sailed through and the emitted comparison cast both sides to characters:
TO_UTF8("a") EQL "a" is FALSE in SEL and answered 1 on MariaDB, MySQL and
SQLite. Refused rather than folded to FALSE -- folding is the road §11.4 closed.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
TO_UTF8("a") EQL "a"
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.bin-is-not-text-comparable-under-in
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"a" IN TO_UTF8("a")
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.two-bins-compare-as-bytes
--- note
This case used to expect both sides cast to CHAR and collated, which is right
for text and wrong for bytes: TO_UTF8(X) EQL TO_UTF8(Y) answered NULL on MariaDB
and MySQL whenever either side was not valid UTF-8, where SEL answers FALSE.
Two BIN operands are already bytes and every dialect here compares bytes
directly. The corpus had exactly one BIN value -- 7ac3a9, valid UTF-8 -- so
nothing could show it until hostile bytes were added.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
TO_UTF8("a") EQL TO_UTF8("a")
--- expect
(CAST('a' AS BINARY) = CAST('a' AS BINARY))
===
### name: kinds.a-number-is-a-text-value
--- note
The other side of the same rule, and the reason the classes are not the static
kinds: spec §4 makes numbers TEXT values, so `1 EQL "1"` is TRUE and NUM vs
TEXT must keep translating.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
1 EQL "1"
--- expect
(CAST(1 AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('1' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: kinds.a-bin-is-not-an-addend
--- note
spec §4: "BOOL and BIN are never numbers". The guard implemented the first half
of that sentence. BLOB + 1 translated, and MariaDB answered 2.0 where MySQL
answered 50 -- two servers, two answers, neither SEL's.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"B": {"kind": "column", "column": "b", "type": "BIN"}}
--- source
B + 1
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.a-bin-cannot-be-negated
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"B": {"kind": "column", "column": "b", "type": "BIN"}}
--- source
-B
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.a-bool-is-not-concatenable
--- note
spec §5.2 ends "BOOL is E_NOT_TEXT". `&` was left out of the arithmetic guard
because it is not arithmetic, and nothing else covered it: FLAG & NAME
concatenated, MariaDB answering '1a' and PostgreSQL 'truea'.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"F": {"kind": "column", "column": "f", "type": "BOOL"}, "N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
F & N
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.a-bin-is-concatenable
--- note
And the half that must keep working: `&` takes text or bytes, and SEL answers
for BIN & TEXT.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"B": {"kind": "column", "column": "b", "type": "BIN"}, "N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
B & N
--- expect
CONCAT(`b`, `n`)
===
### name: kinds.a-bool-is-not-a-function-argument
--- note
Nothing checked function arguments at all. Measured on MariaDB: 26 functions
accepted a BOOL and 26 accepted a BIN where SEL raises. This is where a column
binding bites and the constant check cannot help -- there is no value to hand
the evaluator, only a declared kind.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"F": {"kind": "column", "column": "f", "type": "BOOL"}}
--- source
UPPER(F)
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.a-bin-is-not-a-text-function-argument
--- note
UPPER(BLOB) answered '1' on three servers and '\X31' on PostgreSQL; LEN(BLOB)
answered 1 on three and 4 on PostgreSQL.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"B": {"kind": "column", "column": "b", "type": "BIN"}}
--- source
LEN(B)
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.a-bin-is-a-byte-function-argument
--- note
The set that does take bytes was measured, not written: every name in
Registry::names() was called with TO_UTF8("a") and with TRUE, and these are the
ones SEL did not raise for. A hand-written table would be the second copy of
SEL's argument rules that §11.4 exists to avoid.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"B": {"kind": "column", "column": "b", "type": "BIN"}}
--- source
BLEN(B)
--- expect
LENGTH(`b`)
===
### name: kinds.isnum-takes-anything
--- note
The one function that accepts a BOOL, because its question is "is this a
number" and every kind has an answer to it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"F": {"kind": "column", "column": "f", "type": "BOOL"}}
--- source
ISNUM(F)
--- expect
(`f` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z')
===
### name: kinds.an-unknown-argument-is-still-the-accepted-limit
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"U": {"kind": "column", "column": "u"}}
--- source
UPPER(U)
--- expect
UPPER(`u`)
===
### name: kinds.bin-against-text-in-a-byte-comparison
--- note
SEL reads both operands of the $ family as bytes, so TO_UTF8("a") $== "a" is
genuinely TRUE. Saying that in SQL means casting the TEXT side to bytes; the
templates cast the BIN side to characters, which is backwards -- PostgreSQL then
compared against the literal '\x61' and answered false, and MySQL answered NULL
for bytes that are not valid UTF-8. Per-dialect byte casts for a comparison
nobody writes is not a trade worth making, so it is refused.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
TO_UTF8("a") $== "a"
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: kinds.bin-against-bin-in-a-byte-comparison
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
TO_UTF8("a") $== TO_UTF8("a")
--- expect
(convert_to(CAST('a' AS TEXT), 'UTF8') = convert_to(CAST('a' AS TEXT), 'UTF8'))
===
### name: review.slot.dollar-pattern-in-an-identifier
--- note
JS's String.prototype.replace treats $$, $&, $`, $' and $1-$9 in the REPLACEMENT
as substitution directives even when the pattern is a plain string, where
Python's str.replace and PHP's str_replace are literal. asCondition() fills the
dialect's isTrue template with rendered SQL, and that SQL carries identifiers the
application chose -- so a column named with $' spliced the template's own tail
back into the output and produced a different condition. Quoted throughout, so
the query still parsed; it just asked about something else.

The column name is deliberately hostile rather than minimal: it is the shape a
reader has to see is INERT once the quoting is right.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- as
condition
--- bindings
{"COL": {"kind": "column", "column": "active$' -- oops"}}
--- source
COL
--- expect
(("active$' -- oops") IS TRUE)
===
### name: review.slot.repeated-in-a-skeleton
--- note
The same line, the other half of the same defect. JS's replace rewrites only the
FIRST occurrence where the other two hosts rewrite every one, so a template
naming {0} twice left the second copy unfilled here and filled there. Registered
rather than shipped, because no shipped dialect repeats a lexical placeholder --
which is exactly why nothing caught it.
--- dialect
twice
--- register
[{"dialect": "twice", "extends": "postgresql", "lexical": {"isTrue": "({0} IS TRUE AND {0} IS NOT NULL)"}}]
--- as
condition
--- bindings
{"COL": {"kind": "column", "column": "c"}}
--- source
COL
--- expect
("c" IS TRUE AND "c" IS NOT NULL)
===
### name: review.slot.repeated-in-a-numeric-wrap
--- note
And the third site: numericLiteral, which sqlite uses to spell a number as text.
A wrap naming {0} twice is legal data and was half-filled.
--- dialect
wrapped
--- register
[{"dialect": "wrapped", "extends": "sqlite", "lexical": {"numericLiteral": "({0} || '' || {0})"}}]
--- source
1
--- expect
(1 || '' || 1)
===
### name: review.lexical.expands-into-itself
--- note
A lexical value may reference another lexical key, and nothing stopped one from
referencing itself. Filling it recursed until the host died -- RangeError on JS,
RecursionError on Python, a host crash through the public API either way, which
is the failure every other guard in this layer exists to prevent.

The cycle is refused rather than a depth capped: the cycle is the actual mistake,
a depth cap would need a number nobody can justify, and with cycles refused the
chain is bounded by the fifteen lexical keys.
--- dialect
selfref
--- register
[{"dialect": "selfref", "extends": "mariadb", "lexical": {"textCast": "X({textCast:0})"}}]
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "column", "column": "a"}}
--- source
A $== "x"
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: review.unify.refusal-carries-a-position
--- note
Every refusal in this layer reports where it happened; this one reported 0:0,
in all three hosts, because the only call that could supply a position passed
none. The position is the character an author has to change, which is the whole
reason the field exists.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
IF(TRUE, TRUE, "A-1")
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:1
===
### name: review.lexical.empty-quote-is-refused
--- note
A quote character that is not a character cannot quote. Left through, the hosts
disagreed about what it meant -- str_replace and Python's str.replace put the
escape between every character AND at each end, the JS host's split/join only
between -- and both answers are nonsense. textCollate is legitimately empty and
still is; these two are not.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"dialect": "noquote", "extends": "ansi", "lexical": {"identQuote": ""}}]
--- source
1
--- throws
LogicException
===
### name: review.binder.parenthesised-is-refused
--- note
The evaluator's rule for an aggregate binder, in Args::symbol, is a bare `var`
node that did NOT come from parentheses -- `(C)` carries the parser's `grouped`
flag, the same flag that makes `(A) = 1` an E_BAD_ASSIGN, and running this is
E_EXPECT_SYMBOL. All three translators tested only the node KIND, so they
accepted the binder and emitted working SQL for a rule that can never run:
`((`x`.`a` > 0) AND (`x`.`b` > 0))`, pushed down for a program the language
refuses.

Accepting where the language refuses is the one direction this layer must never
fail in. Refusing is also the useful outcome: tryTranslate returns null, the
application evaluates locally instead, and the evaluation raises the real error.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"V": {"kind": "columns", "items": [{"table": "x", "column": "a", "type": "NUM"}, {"table": "x", "column": "b", "type": "NUM"}]}}
--- source
ALL(V, (C), C > 0)
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:9
===
### name: review.binder.bare-name-still-works
--- note
The same rule one character shorter, pinning that the fix refuses the
parentheses and not the three-argument form itself.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"V": {"kind": "columns", "items": [{"table": "x", "column": "a", "type": "NUM"}, {"table": "x", "column": "b", "type": "NUM"}]}}
--- source
ALL(V, C, C > 0)
--- as
condition
--- expect
((`x`.`a` > 0) AND (`x`.`b` > 0))
===
